</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>State the Welch confidence interval for a difference of means.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>State the paired confidence interval.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>State the large-sample interval for a difference of proportions.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>State the chi-square pivot used for a normal variance interval.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>\overline{x}=40,
\qquad
\sigma=8,
\qquad
n=64.</md>
</p>

<p>Construct a <m>95\%</m> confidence interval for <m>\mu</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For the same data, construct a <m>99\%</m> confidence interval and compare
its width with the <m>95\%</m> interval.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>n=36,
\qquad
\overline{x}=25,
\qquad
s=6.</md>
</p>

<p>Write the <m>95\%</m> one-sample <m>t</m>-interval in terms of the appropriate
critical value.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>n=500,
\qquad
x=300.</md>
</p>

<p>Construct the large-sample Wald <m>95\%</m> confidence interval for <m>p</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For the previous data, write the Wilson interval formula and compare its
center with <m>\widehat{p}</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the required sample size to estimate a mean with margin of error at
most <m>3</m>, assuming</p>

<p>
<md>\sigma=15</md>
</p>

<p>and <m>95\%</m> confidence.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the conservative sample size required to estimate a population
proportion with margin of error <m>0.04</m> at <m>95\%</m> confidence.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>\overline{x}_1=20,
\quad
s_1=5,
\quad
n_1=30,</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\overline{x}_2=17,
\quad
s_2=4,
\quad
n_2=35.</md>
</p>

<p>Write the Welch confidence interval for <m>\mu_1-\mu_2</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Suppose paired differences have</p>

<p>
<md>\overline{d}=2.5,
\qquad
s_D=4,
\qquad
n=25.</md>
</p>

<p>Construct the <m>95\%</m> paired interval in terms of the appropriate
<m>t</m>-critical value.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>\widehat{p}_1=0.55,
\quad
n_1=200,</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\widehat{p}_2=0.45,
\quad
n_2=250.</md>
</p>

<p>Construct a <m>95\%</m> interval for <m>p_1-p_2</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the known-<m>\sigma</m> interval for a normal mean by inverting the
standard normal pivot.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the one-sample <m>t</m>-interval from the <m>t</m>-pivot.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that increasing <m>n</m> by a factor of <m>4</m> halves the known-<m>\sigma</m>
margin of error.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the sample-size formula</p>

<p>
<md>n
=
\left(
\frac{
z^*\sigma
}{E}
\right)^2.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that</p>

<p>
<md>p(1-p)</md>
</p>

<p>is maximized at</p>

<p>
<md>p=\frac12.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the conservative sample-size formula for a proportion by using</p>

<p>
<md>p=0.5.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why the Welch interval is often preferred to the pooled interval.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the pooled variance estimator from the two independent sample sums
of squares.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why a paired interval uses the standard deviation of the
differences rather than separate standard deviations.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the large-sample standard error for the difference of two
independent sample proportions.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive a confidence interval for a normal variance from the chi-square
pivot.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why the variance interval is asymmetric.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive a confidence interval for the ratio of two normal variances using
the <m>F</m>-distribution.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show how a monotone transformation converts a confidence interval for
<m>\theta</m> into an interval for <m>g(\theta)</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Use the delta method to construct an approximate confidence interval for</p>

<p>
<md>\log\theta.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why likelihood-ratio intervals may be asymmetric.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain the difference between a Wald interval and a score interval.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Construct a basic bootstrap interval from given bootstrap quantiles.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Compare percentile and basic bootstrap confidence intervals.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why bootstrapping cannot repair a nonrepresentative sample.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain the duality between a two-sided <m>1-\alpha</m> confidence interval
and a level-<m>\alpha</m> hypothesis test.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why comparing two separate group confidence intervals is not the
same as constructing an interval for their difference.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Explain why a narrow confidence interval does not guarantee an unbiased
